.page gzip 1 compress files
.alias gunzip

# SYNOPSIS
*gzip* [*-cdfklnNqrtv1-9*] [_FILE_...]
*gunzip* [*-cfklnqrtv*] [_FILE_...]

# DESCRIPTION
`gzip` replaces each _FILE_ with a compressed copy named _FILE_.gz that keeps the owner's permissions and the modification time, and removes the original. `gunzip` (or *gzip -d*) does the opposite: _FILE_.gz becomes _FILE_ again, with the time stored inside the archive. Files that end in .tgz come back as .tar.

The compression is DEFLATE from libz: LZ77 with hash chains, Huffman coding, and stored blocks for data that does not compress. Several gzip members glued together are read as one stream. With no _FILE_ (or with *-*) the data comes from standard input and goes to standard output.

# OPTIONS
@ *-c*
    Write to standard output and keep the original files.
@ *-d*
    Decompress.
@ *-f*
    Overwrite an existing output file, and compress even when the output is a terminal.
@ *-k*
    Keep the original file.
@ *-l*
    List the compressed size, the uncompressed size, the ratio and the CRC-32 of each file.
@ *-n*, *-N*
    Do not store (*-n*) or do store (*-N*, the default) the original name and time.
@ *-q*
    Say nothing about warnings.
@ *-r*
    Walk directories and treat every file below them.
@ *-t*
    Test: decompress and check the checksums, write nothing.
@ *-v*
    Say what was done to each file.
@ *-1* ... *-9*
    Speed against size. *-1* is the fastest, *-9* the smallest, *-6* is the default.

# EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 for an error (a damaged or truncated file is an error), 2 for a warning such as a file that was ignored.

# EXAMPLES
    gzip notes.txt
    gunzip -c notes.txt.gz | head
    tar -cf - docs | gzip -9 > docs.tar.gz

# SEE ALSO
zcat(1), crc32(1)
